{
"slug": "reo.html", "fr_slug": "reo.html", "cat": "Bouyon artists", "nav": "Reo",
"title": "Reo: the Prince of Bouyon, from Joli BB to WCMF 2026",
"desc": "Reo, a Dominican bouyon artist billed as the Prince of Bouyon. Joli BB with Maurane Voyer, La Vie Doux, his 2026 Caribbean Music Awards nominations and WCMF.",
"h1": "Reo: the face of the new Dominican generation",
"intro": "Reo is a Dominican bouyon artist, billed as the Prince of Bouyon. With Joli BB, a duet with Martinican singer Maurane Voyer, and a 2026 Caribbean Music Awards nomination, he embodies the new Dominican generation building an international career with the French Antilles, while keeping Dominica in view.",
"sections": [
("Who Reo is: a stage name, a writer name", ["Reo is the artist name of a bouyon singer from Dominica. His real name, Dario Yankey, appears in the songwriting credits of his tracks. That double identity is not a footnote: it is a reminder that behind the stage name circulating on posters and social media, there is a writer, credited as such on his songs. On this site, credits matter as much as choruses, because bouyon has too often travelled without them.", "Reo is billed as the Prince of Bouyon. As with the other crowns of the genre, that title is attributed: it is the billing used by 13th Street Promotions. Presenting it this way, with its attribution, says two things at once. On one hand, a promotional machine from the scene chose that royal title to position him, which signals ambition and a claimed status in the genre succession. On the other hand, the title remains subjective: this site reports it, it does not award it.", "The contrast with the previous generation helps to place Reo. Where <a href=\"/en/asa-bantan.html\">Asa Bantan</a> is widely called the King of Bouyon from Grand Bay, Reo arrives with the prince label and a path already turned outward: collaborations with the French Antilles, Caribbean nominations, and the big Roseau festival stage. The royal vocabulary belongs to the scene itself, and it draws a line of succession: Dominican bouyon has its established figures and its named heir generation.", "Our dossier presents Reo as the face of the new Dominican generation. Face means he concentrates what defines that generation: working from Dominica, collaborating with Martinique and Guadeloupe, aiming for the stages and awards of the whole Caribbean, and existing on the platforms where bouyon lives its second life. The following sections follow that path through his verified tracks and dates."]),
("Joli BB: the collaboration that launched him in public", ["The track that installed Reo with this site’s audience is Joli BB, with Martinican singer Maurane Voyer. The track was released on October 9, 2026 on the Mauve label and runs 2 minutes 43. It was written by Maurane Voyer, Dario Yankey and Nicolas Lanclume. Those credits, published and verifiable, are repeated here exactly: two artists from two islands, Dominica and Martinique, and a third writer on the record.", "The structure of the track tells the era. A Dominica and Martinique collaboration is no longer an exceptional meeting organised for a festival: it is a commercial release, on a label, with shared writing credits. Bouyon has always circulated between islands, since the days when it arrived in Guadeloupe and Martinique as jump up after its creation by <a href=\"/en/wck.html\">WCK</a>. Joli BB shows that circulation turned into a working method for the young generation.", "Bouyon Shattaz published Joli BB on the very day of its release. That editorial choice is part of Reo’s dossier on this site: the media follows the artist in real time, at the moment tracks come out, and not only once they have become obvious. For an artist building internationally, that responsiveness matters: it documents the trajectory as it happens, with the dates, labels and credits of the day.", "Joli BB is also an ideal entry point for a French-speaking listener. The Martinican voice of Maurane Voyer meets the Dominican bouyon of Reo, in a short, direct format. Find the track in our <a href=\"/en/playlist-bouyon.html\">bouyon playlist</a>, among the other releases that draw the sound of 2026."]),
("La Vie Doux, Under Pressure, Still Love Me: the recent catalogue", ["Around Joli BB, Reo’s recent catalogue includes La Vie Doux, Under Pressure and Still Love Me. Those three tracks, cited in our dossier as his recent tracks, show an artist in regular production, not the singer of a single viral clip. For a scene like bouyon, where many careers are played out over carnival seasons, that regularity of release is an important professional signal.", "La Vie Doux holds a particular place in that catalogue. The track, performed with Tehilah, was nominated for Bouyon Song of the Year at the 2026 Caribbean Music Awards. A song nomination, distinct from the artist nomination discussed below, means that Reo’s writing and performance work was selected, track by track, by a Caribbean institution. For a reader discovering the artist, La Vie Doux is therefore priority listening: it is the track the awards singled out.", "Under Pressure and Still Love Me complete the portrait. Without inventing descriptions our dossier does not contain, this profile presents them for what they are in Reo’s documented path: recent tracks that feed his stages, his playlists and his presence with Dominican and Antillean audiences. They appear, with Joli BB and La Vie Doux, in the structured data of this page, as official listening landmarks for his profile.", "Taken together, those four tracks draw a readable strategy: collaborations that open audiences, with Maurane Voyer and with Tehilah, and tracks under his own name that install an identity. That is the working method of the new Dominican generation, and Reo is one of its clearest illustrations in 2026."]),
("The 2026 Caribbean Music Awards: Caribbean recognition", ["In 2026, Reo was nominated at the Caribbean Music Awards in the Bouyon Artist of the Year category. The very name of the category deserves attention: at a Caribbean ceremony of that scale, there is a bouyon category. The genre born in Roseau in 1988 with <a href=\"/en/wck.html\">WCK</a> now has its own regional awards, and Reo is among the artists selected to represent the bouyon year.", "To that artist nomination is added that of La Vie Doux, with Tehilah, for Bouyon Song of the Year at the same awards. Being nominated twice, as artist and as song, in the same edition strengthens the signal: this is not a symbolic presence, but a year recognised in both of its dimensions, the performer and the track.", "This profile reports nominations, and only nominations, because that is what our dossier verifies. It does not turn a nomination into a win and adds no undocumented result. That precision is part of this site’s contract with its readers: published facts are those of the dossiers, attributed when they must be, and dated when they can be.", "For Dominica, those nominations carry a collective value. Every Dominican artist nominated in a bouyon category recalls, in front of the whole Caribbean, which island the genre comes from. Reo carries that reminder with a prince billing and French collaborations: a very 2026 way of defending the origin, through visibility rather than speeches."]),
("WCMF 2026 and what follows: building from Dominica", ["According to published World Creole Music Festival 2026 guides, Reo plays Night 2, Saturday October 24, 2026. He shares that night, according to those same guides, with <a href=\"/en/asa-bantan.html\">Asa Bantan</a>. The WCMF stage, in Roseau, is Dominica’s greatest Creole showcase: playing there places Reo at the centre of his island, on the same bill as the established figures of the genre, in front of the audience that watched him grow.", "That date sums up the balance our dossier attributes to Reo: building an international career with French Antilles collaborations, while keeping Dominica in view. International, in his case, does not mean leaving and disappearing from the local landscape. It means circulating, collaborating, being nominated, then coming back to play the big night in Roseau. That back-and-forth is what makes the new bouyon careers solid.", "Bouyon Shattaz follows Reo as the face of that new generation. The media published Joli BB on its release day and continues to document his tracks, nominations and stages on this site and in the <a href=\"/en/playlist-bouyon.html\">bouyon playlist</a>. For French-speaking readers, Reo is one of the shortest paths to Dominican bouyon today: a prince billed by his scene, short collaborative tracks, and an island that stays at the centre of the story.", "Why Reo matters, in short: because he proves Dominican bouyon can produce a new generation that speaks to the French Antilles and the whole Caribbean without renouncing its address. Start with Joli BB, continue with La Vie Doux, then look at Night 2 of WCMF 2026: in three stops, you will have the complete portrait of the generation arriving."]),
("Listening to Reo: where to start", ["To discover Reo, the simplest order follows the order of this profile. Start with Joli BB, the meeting track with Maurane Voyer, because it contains the artist’s whole project in 2 minutes 43: Dominica and Martinique in the same track, shared writing credits, a label, Mauve, and a release that Bouyon Shattaz documented on the very day, October 9, 2026. A listener who has never heard Reo hears immediately what the new generation does differently there: it records from the outset at the scale of the French Antilles, without waiting for local consecration to collaborate.", "Continue with La Vie Doux, with Tehilah, the track nominated for Bouyon Song of the Year at the 2026 Caribbean Music Awards. Hearing it after Joli BB lets you compare two collaborations and understand why the Caribbean awards selected this one. Then add Under Pressure and Still Love Me, the other recent tracks in the dossier, to hear Reo outside the duet format and measure the regularity of his current production. Those four tracks form the official listening path of this page: they are repeated in its structured data and in our <a href=\"/en/playlist-bouyon.html\">bouyon playlist</a>.", "Finish the path with context. Re-read the genre journey, from <a href=\"/en/wck.html\">WCK</a> to the current branches, on our <a href=\"/en/history.html\">history of bouyon</a> page, then look at where Reo stands on Night 2 of the World Creole Music Festival 2026, Saturday October 24, 2026, alongside <a href=\"/en/asa-bantan.html\">Asa Bantan</a>. That context is not a decorative extra: it says exactly what Bouyon Shattaz defends with Reo, a Dominican new generation documented track by track, credit by credit, stage by stage, so that his international career grows with his island rather than beside it. That is the standard this site will keep applying to every new Reo release, nomination and stage it documents from here on."]),
],
"faq": [("Why is Reo called the Prince of Bouyon?", "Reo is billed as the Prince of Bouyon by 13th Street Promotions. It is an attributed billing, a subjective title from the scene, reported here with its attribution."), ("Who wrote Joli BB?", "Joli BB, by Reo with Maurane Voyer, released on October 9, 2026 on the Mauve label, was written by Maurane Voyer, Dario Yankey and Nicolas Lanclume. Dario Yankey is Reo’s real name."), ("What are Reo nominations in 2026?", "Reo was nominated for Bouyon Artist of the Year at the 2026 Caribbean Music Awards, and his track La Vie Doux, with Tehilah, was nominated for Bouyon Song of the Year at the same awards.")],
"date_published": "2026-09-16",
"extra_graph": [{"@type": "ProfilePage", "@id": "https://bouyonmusique.com/en/reo.html#profile", "mainEntity": {"@type": "Person", "@id": "https://bouyonmusique.com/en/reo.html#artist", "name": "Reo", "description": "Dominican bouyon artist, billed as the Prince of Bouyon.", "genre": "Bouyon", "homeLocation": {"@type": "Place", "name": "Dominica"}, "sameAs": ["https://www.instagram.com/reookboom/"], "track": [{"@type": "MusicRecording", "name": "Joli BB"}, {"@type": "MusicRecording", "name": "La Vie Doux"}, {"@type": "MusicRecording", "name": "Under Pressure"}, {"@type": "MusicRecording", "name": "Still Love Me"}]}}],
},
{
"slug": "smokiller.html", "fr_slug": "smokiller.html", "cat": "Bouyon artists", "nav": "Smokiller",
"title": "Smokiller: the producer who flips hits into bouyon",
"desc": "Smokiller, Dominican-born artist and producer, founder of Bouyon Shattaz: bouyon remixes, Throw Dat, Nothing Into Something and radio servicing.",
"h1": "Smokiller: the hits of the world, the recipe of Dominica",
"intro": "Smokiller is a Dominican-born artist and producer, founder of Bouyon Shattaz. Born Dominican and now American, Dominican-born, he takes Afrobeats, pop, rap and R&B hits and flips them into bouyon versions, with the keyboards, tempo and energy of the genre born in Roseau.",
"sections": [
("Who Smokiller is: the man, the brand, the founder", ["Smokiller is a Dominican-born artist and producer. It is not a decorative pseudonym: it is the name under which he produces, remixes, publishes and signs his work, from YouTube to TikTok, from his site smokiller.com to the Bouyon Shattaz platform he founded. BouyonMusique.com, the site you are reading, is published by Bouyon Shattaz: Smokiller is therefore both an artist documented by this site and the man behind the house that publishes it, which this profile states openly.", "His identity must be told in the right words. He was born Dominican and is now American. The wording used on this site is Dominican-born. His bloodline and culture never left him, and they are who he is. This site refers to him as Smokiller, following his own way of presenting himself in his articles and projects.", "That identity explains his musical project. A producer born in Dominica, living his life as an American, who listens to hits from all over the world and hears, in each of them, a possible bouyon version: that is the starting point of Smokiller. It is not about imitating the scenes that succeed, but about bringing their tracks back to the recipe of his island, the one <a href=\"/en/wck.html\">WCK</a> created in Roseau in 1988.", "Smokiller combines several trades that this profile details in turn: producer for other artists, remixer of international hits, curator of a free download catalogue on smokiller.com, founder of a media and of a distribution and rights project, and head of radio servicing to Caribbean stations. Few actors in current bouyon bring together so many functions. That is what makes this page the longest of the artist profiles on this site."]),
("The Smokiller method: flipping a hit into bouyon", ["The method is simple to state and demanding to execute. Smokiller takes Afrobeats, pop, rap and R&B hits and flips them into bouyon versions, with bouyon keyboards, bouyon tempo and bouyon energy. Three elements therefore change in the flip: the instrumentation, carried by the keyboards that have signed the genre since its creation; the speed, set to the tempo of the Dominican fete; and the energy, that of a music built to make a crowd answer.", "That work sits notably in the wrecketeng style. Wrecketeng is Dominica sampled-melody, rap-vocal style, one of the bouyon branches born from the spoken delivery of the origins. Our dedicated page on <a href=\"/en/wrecketeng.html\">wrecketeng</a> explains that lineage. Working in that style, for a remixer, means owning the sample as material and the voice as engine, two gestures at the heart of the Smokiller method.", "A successful bouyon remix is not just about speeding a track up. If the audience recognises the original hit but dances to another machine, the flip has worked. The keyboards must carry the melody in the language of bouyon, the tempo must install the fete without breaking the track, and the energy must stay that of the genre: direct, festive, made for call and response. It is arrangement and production work, not a filter applied in a hurry.", "The method also has a cultural function. Every world hit flipped into bouyon demonstrates, in practice, that the Dominican genre can welcome any melody of the moment. For audiences discovering bouyon through a track they already know, the remix is a doorway. For Dominica, every successful flip is a reminder of address: that energy has a name, bouyon, and an origin, Roseau."]),
("Producer: Klass Money Throw Dat (Bouyon 2026)", ["Smokiller does not only remix: he also produces for other artists. He produced Klass Money’s track Throw Dat (Bouyon 2026). That production credit, distinct from his remix catalogue, shows the other face of his work: building a bouyon track for another artist’s voice, in the year of the genre that the title itself displays, Bouyon 2026.", "The track appears in a Muse page short, which gives it an extra published trace beyond the usual platforms. For this site, that detail matters less than the credit itself: the name of Smokiller as producer of another artist track installs his signature in the 2026 bouyon catalogue, alongside his own releases.", "Producing for others also changes a musician’s place in a scene. The remixer works on existing works; the producer takes part in the birth of new works under other artists’ names. Smokiller holds both positions, which strengthens his role in the Bouyon Shattaz ecosystem: the same ear that flips world hits produces the original tracks of the scene he documents.", "Throw Dat (Bouyon 2026) stands at the head of the tracks listed in the structured data of this page, before the remixes. That order is not a popularity ranking: it simply recalls that the Smokiller dossier begins with an original production credit, and that the flips that follow are the work of a producer, not just a fan of quick edits."]),
("The Coi Leray moment: Nothing Into Something and Players", ["The moment that moved Smokiller to another scale is his bouyon flip of Coi Leray Nothing Into Something. Posted on September 20, 2026, the flip reached 854.7K TikTok views, with 29.9K likes. Those figures come from screenshots shared by Smokiller himself on October 19, 2026. This profile reports them with that source and that date, as it reports every measurement: attributed, dated, without inflation.", "To those numbers is added a gesture worth more than a counter: Coi Leray reposted the flip. When the original artist reposts the flip of her own track, the remix stops being a fan exercise and becomes a validated meeting between two scenes. For bouyon, a genre used to travelling without always being credited, that repost is an event: the Dominican version of an American hit is shown, by its very performer, to her own audience.", "The story continued off social media. A Dominica News Online article about this flip was drafted in October 2026. The island press telling the success of a Dominican-born producer on a world platform: the loop of the Smokiller project can be read in that journey. The track of an American artist becomes a subject of Dominican media pride, because the flipping work itself is Dominican.", "The Smokiller Coi Leray catalogue does not stop there. His remix of Players, another Coi Leray track, is published on his YouTube channel. The two tracks draw a sustained relationship with the repertoire of a single artist: a first flip that explodes on TikTok and draws a repost, a second that installs the work over time, on the long-form video platform. Readers can find those tracks via smokiller.com and this site <a href=\"/en/playlist-bouyon.html\">bouyon playlist</a>."]),
("smokiller.com: the links hub and the free downloads", ["The centre of Smokiller’s direct distribution is smokiller.com, his links hub. The site offers free downloads that show the breadth of the catalogue: the Coi Leray Nothing Into Something remix in WAV, the Shenseea Vulnerability Bouyon Shattaz remix in WAV, and the Ayra Starr Heaven Baby Bouyon Shattaz remix in M4A. The formats matter: WAV is the format of DJs and radios, the format of serious work in the booth and in programming.", "Alongside those three featured downloads, the hub also offers instrumentals and remix videos: Bryson Tiller with Outside, Loe Shimmy, Kalash x Kronix, RnBouyon and Shake It To The Max. That list, as it appears in our dossier, draws the perimeter of Smokiller’s listening: American rap and R&B, voices of the Caribbean and its diasporas, dance tracks of the moment. Everything that passes through his hands comes out with bouyon keyboards.", "The choice of free download is a circulation strategy. A DJ who downloads a WAV plays it; a radio that receives a clean file programmes it; an audience that downloads shares. In the bouyon economy, where value is first built in fetes and programming, putting the files into the right hands is worth more than a paywall. smokiller.com is the tool of that policy, simple and direct.", "The three downloads also tell the alliances of the catalogue: Coi Leray for American rap, Shenseea for the Caribbean voice, Ayra Starr for global Afrobeats. Heaven Baby, the third, also has a particular function in the Bouyon Shattaz project, detailed in the next section. Vulnerability and Heaven Baby carry the Bouyon Shattaz Remix mention, the house signature, where the Coi Leray flip carries the TikTok moment of the catalogue."]),
("Heaven Baby, the Bouyon Shattaz campaign and radio servicing", ["The bouyon remix of Ayra Starr Heaven Baby anchors a 30-day Instagram follower-growth campaign for the @bouyonshattaz account, launched on October 19, 2026. Anchoring a media campaign on a producer remix says the nature of the Bouyon Shattaz project: the content that grows the audience is the music itself, produced by the founder, and not decorative content around it.", "Bouyon Shattaz is Smokiller’s media platform and his distribution and rights project. The media documents bouyon, this site is its French and English library, and the distribution and rights side works so that Dominican artists keep the benefit of their genre, in the spirit of the defence of the origin told on our <a href=\"/en/history.html\">history of bouyon</a> page. Smokiller is therefore not only an artist in the catalogue: he is the operator of the system that distributes it.", "That system includes active radio servicing. Smokiller runs the supply of Bouyon Shattaz rotation packs to Caribbean stations: the stations receive the tracks in usable formats, including the WAV remixes from his hub, for their programming and their DJs. Servicing connects studio work to field work: a remix flipped anywhere has bouyon value only if it ends up played, in Roseau, in the Antilles and across the whole region.", "Why Smokiller matters, in short: because he built, around a simple method, flipping world hits into bouyon, a complete system running from production to media, from free download to radio. Dominican-born, founder of Bouyon Shattaz, producer of Throw Dat (Bouyon 2026), author of a Coi Leray flip reposted by the artist and seen 854.7K times on TikTok according to his own screenshots: his dossier is that of a builder as much as a remixer. Listen to his tracks in the <a href=\"/en/playlist-bouyon.html\">bouyon playlist</a>, then go through smokiller.com for the files."]),
("Listening to Smokiller: the catalogue path", ["The Smokiller catalogue is best heard in an order that tells his method. Start with the production credit, Klass Money Throw Dat (Bouyon 2026), to hear the producer before the remixer: an original track, built for another artist, in the year the genre itself displays. That starting point recalls that the flips that follow are not amateur edits, but the work of a producer who knows how to build a bouyon track from zero for a given voice.", "Move next to the Coi Leray moment, in the order of our dossier: Nothing Into Something first, the flip of September 20, 2026 reposted by the artist and seen 854.7K times on TikTok according to Smokiller’s screenshots, then Players, the remix published on his YouTube channel. Heard back to back, the two tracks show the two tempos of his work: immediate impact on the short platform, then installation over time on the long platform. Between the two, the same gesture repeats: bouyon keyboards, fete tempo and the genre energy applied to an American rap repertoire.", "Add the two Bouyon Shattaz remixes available for download on smokiller.com: Shenseea Vulnerability, in WAV, for the Caribbean voice of the catalogue, and Ayra Starr Heaven Baby, in M4A, for its global Afrobeats side. Heaven Baby deserves careful listening: it is the track anchoring the 30-day @bouyonshattaz Instagram campaign launched on October 19, 2026, so it is the exact point where Smokiller studio work becomes the engine of the Bouyon Shattaz media. Finish with the instrumentals and videos on the hub, from Bryson Tiller and Outside to Shake It To The Max, to hear the method bare, without the original voice.", "That path has a practical destination: radio and the fete. The WAV files on the hub are designed for DJs and programming, and the Bouyon Shattaz rotation pack servicing sends this catalogue to Caribbean stations. Listening to Smokiller all the way through therefore means following a track from its original form, an Afrobeats, pop, rap or R&B hit, to its complete bouyon life: flipped in the studio, downloaded by DJs, programmed by radios and danced to by the genre’s audiences."]),
],
"faq": [("Who is Smokiller?", "Smokiller is a Dominican-born artist and producer, founder of Bouyon Shattaz. Born Dominican and now American, Dominican-born, he flips Afrobeats, pop, rap and R&B hits into bouyon versions."), ("Which Smokiller remix did Coi Leray repost?", "His bouyon flip of Nothing Into Something, posted on September 20, 2026, reached 854.7K TikTok views and 29.9K likes according to his own screenshots of October 19, 2026, and Coi Leray reposted the flip."), ("Where can I download Smokiller remixes?", "On smokiller.com, his links hub, as free downloads: the Coi Leray Nothing Into Something remix in WAV, Shenseea Vulnerability in WAV and Ayra Starr Heaven Baby in M4A, plus instrumentals and remix videos.")],
"date_published": "2026-09-15",
"extra_graph": [{"@type": "ProfilePage", "@id": "https://bouyonmusique.com/en/smokiller.html#profile", "mainEntity": {"@type": "Person", "@id": "https://bouyonmusique.com/en/smokiller.html#artist", "name": "Smokiller", "description": "Dominican-born producer who flips Afrobeats, pop, rap and R&B hits into bouyon.", "genre": "Bouyon", "birthPlace": {"@type": "Place", "name": "Dominica"}, "sameAs": ["https://www.youtube.com/@smo.killer", "https://www.instagram.com/smo.killer/", "https://www.tiktok.com/@smo.killer", "https://smokiller.com/"], "track": [{"@type": "MusicRecording", "name": "Throw Dat (Bouyon 2026)"}, {"@type": "MusicRecording", "name": "Players (Bouyon Remix)"}, {"@type": "MusicRecording", "name": "Nothing Into Something (Bouyon Remix)"}, {"@type": "MusicRecording", "name": "Vulnerability (Bouyon Shattaz Remix)"}, {"@type": "MusicRecording", "name": "Heaven Baby (Bouyon Shattaz Remix)"}]}}],
},
